  "items": [
    {
      "external_id": "C12-BIO-CH22-22.2.6-FORMULA",
      "chapter": "pk-punjab/xii/biology/c12-bio/ch22",
      "topic": "pk-punjab/xii/biology/c12-bio/ch22/u-formula~6",
      "title": "Formula",
      "language": "en",
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "source_document_id": "779562c7-0658-5de6-b1a6-2ee025d3b016",
          "pdf_from": 196,
          "pdf_to": 197,
          "note": "Formula: a main subheading of 22.2.6 Percentile, its own page (owner 2026-11-04); PDF 196-197."
        }
      ],
      "body": {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "R = \\frac{P}{100}\\,(n + 1)",
            "text_alt": "R = P/100 (n + 1)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Where"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "R = rank"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "P = desired percentile"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "n = number of observations in the data set"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "If R is an integer, the value at the Rth position in the ordered dataset is the percentile. If R is not an integer (e.g., 4.4), interpolation between the two nearest ranks is used to estimate the percentile value. For example, if it lies between 4th and 5th data points, percentile value="
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\text{Value at } 4^{\\text{th}} + 0.4 \\times (\\text{value at } 5^{\\text{th}} - \\text{value at } 4^{\\text{th}})",
            "text_alt": "Value at 4th + 0.4 × (value at 5th − value at 4th)"
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 4,
            "text": "Steps to calculate percentile with example"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Suppose we have the following dataset representing the heights (cm) of 10 individuals: 160, 162, 168, 165, 170, 175, 172, 178, 180, 182. To calculate the 40th percentile:"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Step 1: Arrange the data in ascending order.",
            "bold": [
              "Step 1:"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "160, 162, 165, 168, 170, 172, 175, 178, 180, 182."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Step 2: Determine the total number of observations.",
            "bold": [
              "Step 2:"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "n = 10"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Step 3: Calculate the rank.",
            "bold": [
              "Step 3:"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "R = P/100 X (n+1)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "R = 40/100 X (10+1) = 4.4"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Step 4: Locate the rank in the dataset and interpolate if necessary.",
            "bold": [
              "Step 4:"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The rank is 4.4, which is not an integer. This means that the 40th percentile lies between 4th (168) and 5th (170) data points."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Interpolate between the 4th and 5th values:"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Percentile value = value at 4th + 0.4 X (value at 5th - value at 4th)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Percentile value = 168 + 0.4 X (170-168) = 168 + 0.4 X 2 = 168 + 0.8 = 168.8"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Step 5: Interpret the result.",
            "bold": [
              "Step 5:"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The 40th percentile value is 168.8. This means that 40% of the observations fall below or equal to 168.8 in this data set."
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
}
